Why the kernel and full extension are both forced
-------------------------------------------------

Each end contains a Klein four: <a,c> in L and <b,e> in R. If H is contained
in a natural classical normalizer K with index-two kernel F, the restriction
of H->K/F to that Klein four has a nonidentity involution in its kernel.
The odd-prime witness also belongs to H0=H intersect F.

For the split case F=SL3(11)=PSL3(11), Gill's complete containing list in
Section3.1, Theorem19 has, at11, only the Singer normalizer399 divisible by19;
that full normalizer is odd. A proper H0 containing both19 and an involution
is therefore impossible. For the unitary case, Montanucci--Zini Section2
(I)--(IV) and Theorem2.1 leave only the projective Singer normalizer111 for37;
its inverse image in SU3(11) has order333, also odd. The order37 witness
survives the central quotient of order3. Surjectivity of H0 onto PSU3 gives
H0 Z=F, and perfectness implies F=[F,F]<=H0; the centre cannot be lost.
Thus in either case H is F or the full K.
Sources: https://arxiv.org/html/math/0603293v3 and
https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.03398 .

The separate three-involution argument excludes F. This is not a fullness
statement for arbitrary generating sets. In the split case two distinct
commuting half-turns x,z give a common diagonal eigenbasis. Write the third
as y=-I+2 ell*u/(u*ell), with u a row, ell a column and u*ell nonzero.
An order19 element is irreducible on the F11 three-space since ord_19(11)=3.
A zero coordinate of u would leave a coordinate hyperplane invariant, and a
zero coordinate of ell would leave its coordinate line invariant. Hence all
coordinates are nonzero. The nonsingular symmetric form with diagonal
ell_i/u_i is preserved by x,y,z. Every determinant-one isometry in dimension3
has eigenvalue1, incompatible with that irreducible19 element. Therefore
this particular three-half-turn subgroup cannot be all inside SL3.

In the unitary case, simultaneous orthogonal eigenvectors of the two
commuting half-turns have nonzero diagonal Hermitian norms in F11. Rescale
coordinates to make every nonzero coordinate of the third positive vector1.
Its reflection formula then has entries in F11, and so do all three marks.
A nonidentity order37 matrix over F11 needs a constituent of degree6 because
ord_37(11)=6; it cannot occur in degree3. Thus this end cannot lie wholly in
SU3. The argument works when some coordinates vanish and does not assume
irreducibility as an extra unverified input in the unitary case.

Consequently the proper ends really are the full SL3(11):2 or SU3(11):2.
The kernel-lower-bound and three-half-turn steps are both essential. Removing
the extension factor2 without a replacement estimate invalidates the claimed
uniform numerical comparison.

